% e) Written explanation:
% The error does not shrink forever because two competing error sources
% are at work. Truncation error (from the terms the central-difference
% formula drops) shrinks as h^2, favoring SMALLER h. But floating-point
% round-off error behaves oppositely: as h shrinks, f(t0+h) and f(t0-h)
% become closer and closer together in value, so their difference loses
% significant digits to rounding, and dividing that increasingly corrupted
% difference by an increasingly tiny 2h amplifies the round-off, favoring
% LARGER h. The observed minimum sits at the h where these two competing
% effects roughly balance -- pushing h any smaller past that point makes
% the round-off term dominate and the total error rises again.

